        "weaviate_id": "001af66f-5d93-4351-b5c7-fd69d0ac56e4",
        "paragraph_text": "Mineralization is associated with veins and breccias in hornfels, which are part of the St Leon Formation. Metallic minerals are represented by pyrite and chalcopyrite. One hole drilled 325 m to the NW returned 2.3% Cu over 2.5 m and 1.2% Cu over 10 m. Mineralization is associated with a fault zone striking at 155. Dionne is located in the same area as number 6 cobalt vein Vronique and Guillaume showings and is described more extensively at item 9.0 entitled Exploration. The Veine  Ren was discovered by EX IN during a Beep Mat survey in 1988. The vein strikes 330 with a sub vertical dip. At its northern extremity, the massive chalcopyrite mineralization is up to 2 m in width, while farther east, the veins splits into 3 smaller veins, about 0.3 m wide and separated by 0.6 to 3 m of waste. Veins are made up of massive chalcopyrite with small amounts of secondary minerals, mainly malachite and azurite. In its north section, the vein is cut by a gabbro diorite sill. The veins are enclosed in a brecciated mudstone. The enclosing rocks were not affected by local metamorphism."
      },
